.TH CHASHMAP 7 "2026-10-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Overview"
.SH NAME
CHASHMAP \- overview of the chashmap hash map
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
A chmap maps keys to values, and an insert, a lookup and a delete take
O(1) time on average. When the map is created it picks one of two
implementations. It uses open addressing when the key and the value are
both integral types of eight bytes or less (long double does not count),
and separate chaining for every other combination and for any map with a
custom key equality procedure. The API is the same either way, and
chmap_create_full(3) describes the selection, the hashing and how the map
resists hash flooding.

The API has two layers. The type-inferred macros, such as
chmap_construct(3), chmap_insert(3), chmap_get(3), chmap_get_ptr(3) and
chmap_remove(3), keep the key type and the value type with the handle,
and on a hard error they stop the program through _ccol_fatal_err(). The
raw functions, chmap_create_full(3), chmap_insert_elem(3),
chmap_get_elem_ref(3), chmap_get_elem_copy(3) and chmap_delete_elem(3),
take every key and value as a cmap_pair and return a ccol_retval_t.
.fi
.SH "OWNERSHIP OF KEYS AND VALUES"
.nf
The map keeps its own copy of every key and value. An insert copies the
bytes of the key and the value into storage the map owns, and an update
frees the old value.

A char * key or value is a NUL-terminated string, and the map copies its
contents together with the terminator. Your own string is yours again as
soon as the insert returns, so you can change or free it without
affecting the map.

chmap_get(3), chmap_get_ptr(3), chmap_get_elem_ref(3) and an iterator
give back string pointers into the map's storage, which you must not
free. Such a pointer is valid until the next insert, delete, reset or
resize of the map, or until the map is destroyed. To replace a stored
string, insert the key again.

A value of any other pointer type, a struct point * for example, is
kept as the pointer itself. The map does not copy, follow or free the
object at that address. That object belongs to the caller and must be
freed before or during the destroy of the map, and
chmap_destroy_with_dtor(3) can run a destructor on each value during
the destroy for that purpose.

An array type such as int[2] or char[16] cannot be the key or value type
of the type-inferred macros. It is a compile error, and the message says
to wrap the array in a struct: C cannot assign an array, so the map
would store the address of the caller's array instead of its elements. A
struct that holds the array works.
.fi
.SH "REFERENCES INTO THE MAP"
.nf
A pointer from chmap_get_ptr(3) or chmap_get_elem_ref(3) is valid until
the map changes, which means an insert, a delete, a reset or a resize. A
lookup invalidates nothing, so you can hold pointers to many values at
once. With both implementations each such pointer is aligned for the
value type.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
A map has no internal lock. You can share one map between threads, but
then every call that changes the map must be serialized against all
other calls on that map.
.fi
